starts with preparation it starts with  really designing an agenda that is  thoughtful I think you should view every  board meeting like an event like a  retreat like you should know what it is  you're trying to accomplish how you want  people to feel during and after the  meeting what you want as the end result  of every different discussion items the  board chair has to be a facilitator all  great board meetings are interactive in  my opinion it's when directors are  talking amongst themselves about  something and the answer the solutions  are coming out is bigger than greater  than any one individual could have  walked in with so that interactive  nature it takes a good board chair to  facilitate that and make sure everyone's  participating sometimes the board chair  has just got to start at his right and  say I want to hear from every single  person you know on this issue and go  right around the room because it's  really an obligation to participate in  those types of meaningful discussions we  often break up into smaller groups and  allow people to talk before we did that  we found that some people sit and let  others do the work the other thing that  has really helped are our board meetings  become more effective is that I'm doing  a lot less reporting we use what's  called a consent agenda and what that  means is that before the board meeting  happens we send out all the updates that  the committees and I want to do and  people are expected to have read them in  advance and the board itself is a  working board meeting where instead of  asking for updates from everybody and  hearing a lot of talking heads we're  actually asking for advice from the  board and digging deep on issues that  face the organization in my own board  meetings we begin with the discussion  the big issues and we actually conclude  the meeting with the fiduciary and legal  oversight pieces so that the maximum  energy is directed towards  forward-thinking opportunities  challenges and we cover the the  necessary for  in legal but it's not preferenced i also  think that every effective board meeting  is evaluated in some format I'm not  talking about a hundred question you  know survey but rather a simple three by  five card where you ask board members  what's the most important thing that we  talked about what's the least important  thing that we talked about and then  finally I guess after an evaluation I  think a good board chair will summarize  the major conclusions of the meeting and  summarize any actions that the group has  agreed on so that when we walk out of  the room we all know what it is we've  agreed on and what we're going to do now  between now and the next court date it's  really important that board members  enjoy the board experience that they  actually have fun together we actually  build in at my organization Social  Venture Partners international we build  in time for the board members to come in  the night before to have dinner together  where frankly we really don't talk about  work that much and but we build  relationships trust friendships we take  that atmosphere into the room the next  morning and people leave the meeting I  hope feeling more stimulated and  energized than when they arrived in it a  board meeting where people leave feeling  drained and tired is a board meeting  that has failed in its purpose
 

